# Patch 0085 — Automation framework + about:workflows (ROADMAP Phase 7;
# PLATFORM_PLAN.md row 4; ADR 0017). The `automation` command domain patch
# 0027 reserved three phases ago finally has something in it.
#
# A WORKFLOW IS DATA, NEVER CODE. There is no script step, no eval, no
# expression language, and there will not be one. A workflow file is
# user-editable JSON that also arrives from imports and, later, from the
# marketplace — "run this arbitrary JS with chrome privileges" is not a
# feature, it is the end of the security model. Every step is one entry from a
# fixed allowlist with typed parameters, validated FAIL-CLOSED against an
# embedded JSON schema (patch 0076's precedent, now applied to a store that
# EXECUTES rather than one that renders) at save AND again at run — the file
# is plain JSON in the profile and can be edited in between.
#
# WHAT A STEP MAY DO IS BOUNDED BY WHAT THE USER COULD DO. Open a page, switch
# Space, close duplicates, snapshot, clip, focus, run a command already in the
# registry. There is deliberately no step that reads a page and sends it
# anywhere, none that writes prefs, and none that touches passwords, cookies
# or permissions — ADR 0011's SDK boundary, held in a second place because
# this is the second surface that could break it. `open-url` is re-checked for
# http(s) beyond the schema: a javascript: or data: URL in an automated
# navigation is the classic way to turn a config file into code execution.
#
# RECURSION IS IMPOSSIBLE BY CONSTRUCTION, not by a depth counter:
# `run-command` refuses any command a workflow registered, and the builder
# does not even offer them. Runs are single-flight per workflow and a second
# trigger during a run is DROPPED, not queued — queueing turns one slow run
# into fifty tabs at once. Steps and tabs-per-run are both capped.
#
# COMMANDS ARE REBUILT FROM WHAT WE REGISTERED, not from the current workflow
# list. Deriving the unregister set from the list is exactly the bug patch
# 0075 fixed in the widget host: delete a workflow and its "Run:" command
# outlives it in the palette forever.
#
# TIMERS DO NOT CATCH UP. An interval workflow runs while Kavacha is open and
# missed windows are not replayed on the next launch. A scheduler that fires
# for the week you were away is a different feature and would astonish anyone
# who closed the browser for a holiday — said in the builder, not just here.
#
# THE BUILDER KNOWS NOTHING ABOUT INDIVIDUAL STEPS. about:workflows renders
# whatever KavachaWorkflowActions declares — a label and typed fields — so a
# new step appears in the UI for free and, more importantly, the page cannot
# offer a step the engine does not implement. That is the failure mode a
# hand-written form eventually ships. Every edit round-trips through save(),
# so there is exactly one place a bad document is refused, and the refusal
# reasons are shown verbatim rather than paraphrased.
#
# The space-switch trigger hooks the same `changeWorkspace` chokepoint as the
# snapshot substrate (ADR 0006) and is fire-and-forget for the same reason: a
# workflow must never make a Space switch wait, and one that throws must never
# leave the switch half-done.
#
